      "text": "Let $r$ be an integer root of the polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0$.\nBy Vieta's formulas, $r$ and each of its conjugates must be roots of the minimal polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7$.\nLet $s$ and $t$ be the other two roots of $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7$.\nThen we have that $r + s + t = -3, rst = -7$.\n\nIf $r$ is one of the roots of a minimal polynomial, then all possible values are $r\\in[-5,5]^C$ (because it would contain the only odd number). Since we are looking for integer values of the coefficient of $x$, we have that $–b$ is an integer, so (after performing polynomial long division ~ 10 times, by other users) we have two possible values for $r$: $r = –1, 0$ because these are the only roots of $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0$ that are odd.\n\nBy Vieta's, we know that $rs + rt + st = –a$. Mustersion, they must be even and one of $s, t$ must be odd because every single addition is $0 \\pmod 2$.\n\n1) If $r = – 1$, then $s + t + st = 7$. The possible values for $s$ are $–1, 2, 3, 6$. If $t = 2$, then $s = 3$. If $t = 3$, then $s = 2$ and $a = – 7$. \\[2 = 2 –( – 1)3\\]\nWe must check $t \\neq 6$ because $a \\neq -15$. If $t = 3 \\cdot 6$, then $s = – 4$ \\[–t = − − 1 )( − 4)\\]\n2) If $r = 0$, then $st = –7$. The possible values for $s, t$ are $–1, 7$ or $–7, 1$. From here, we see that $a = 3$. And obviously $a = 20$ is also a possible value.\n\nSo, the final answer is \\boxed{-1,0, 3, 20}",
