      "text": "Given the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\), we need to find all integer values of \\(a\\) such that the polynomial has at least one integer root. Let's denote the integer root by \\(r\\). Then the polynomial can be factored as follows:\n\n\\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = (x - r)(x^2 + px + q)\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[x^3 + px^2 + qx - rx^2 - rpx - rq = x^3 + (p-r)x^2 + (q-rpx)\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients with the original polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7\\), we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n1. \\(p - r = 3\\)\n2. \\(q - r = a\\)\n3. \\(0 = 7\\)\n\nFrom the third equation, we see that it is trivially satisfied, so we can focus on the first two equations. Solving the first equation for \\(p\\):\n\n\\[p = r + 3\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(p = r + 3\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[q - r = a\\]\n\nThus, we have \\(q = r + a + 3\\). Now we need to consider the possible integer values for \\(r\\) that make \\(q\\) an integer. Since \\(q\\) must be an integer, \\(r\\) can be any integer because \\(q = r + a + 3\\) will be an integer if \\(r\\) is an integer. Therefore, \\(r\\) can be any integer.\n\nNext, we need to ensure that the quadratic \\(x^2 + (r + 3)x + (r + a + 3)\\) has real roots, which it will if its discriminant is non-negative. The discriminant of \\(x^2 + (r + 3)x + (r + a + 3)\\) is:\n\n\\[(r + 3)^2 - 4(r + a + 3) = r^2 + 6r + 9 - 4r - 4a - 12 = r^2 + 2r - 4a - 3\\]\n\nWe need this discriminant to be non-negative for \\(r\\) to have real roots. Setting the discriminant to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n\\[r^2 + 2r - 4a - 3 \\geq 0\\]\n\nFor any integer \\(r\\), this inequality will be satisfied because \\(r^2 + 2r\\) is a quadratic expression that can take values in all non-negative regions. However, we need to check if there are specific values of \\(a\\) that make this inequality hold for all \\(r\\). This happens if the coefficient of \\(r^2\\) is negative, which it is not. Instead, we can find a specific \\(a\\) that works for all \\(r\\) by noting that the inequality must hold for the average value of \\(r^2 + 2r\\) over all integers or by choosing a specific \\(a\\) that simplifies the expression. \n\nLet's choose \\(a = -1\\):\n\n\\[r^2 + 2r - 4(-1) - 3 = r^2 + 2r + 1 = (r + 1)^2\\]\n\nFor any integer \\(r\\), \\((r + 1)^2 \\geq 0\\), so the inequality holds. Therefore, \\(a = -1\\) is the only value that satisfies the conditions. \n\nThus, the only integer value of \\(a\\) that allows the polynomial to have an integer root is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{-1}\\]",
